  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe remnant low associated with Isaias has taken a track that \nis farther to the east than previously forecast, through eastern\nTennessee towards the Central Appalachians. The axis of deep \nmoisture is to the east of the track and is primarily focused \nacross West Virginia and Pennsylvania which is where the heavier\nrain is going to occur. Rainfall overnight has been quite \nlight, with a quarter inch or less and many areas receiving a \ntenth of an inch or less. Areas located east of I-77 could still\nsee an additional half inch of rain while amounts are going to \nrange from 0.10-0.25 elsewhere. The upper level closed low is \ndisplaced to the west of the surface low and will track \nnortheast through the Ohio Valley today. This will maintain some\nshower activity but dry air in the mid levels will be a \nlimiting factor and cut-back significantly on rainfall amounts.\n\n\nSome locations across northeast Ohio and Northwest pennsylvania are \nstarting to see winds gusting around 20 mph where the gradient is \nthe tightening up. We still believe a few stronger gusts could \noccur for northern portions of Erie Pennsylvania where the \nsoutheasterly wind direction is favorable for enhanced downsloping.\nGusts of 30-40 mph remain possible through mid morning. Abundant\ncloud cover is expected today and temperatures will peak in the\n60s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nAn upper level ridge quickly builds north behind the departing \ntrough on Monday. At the surface, a ridge will build east across the \narea with southwesterly winds and warm advection kicking in \nacross Northwest Ohio during the afternoon. Highs on Monday \nrange from about 70 in the east to 80 in the west. The \nanomalously warm airmass airmass with 850mb temperatures of \nnearly 20C will be in place on Tuesday ahead of a cold front \nsettling south across the region. Highs on Tuesday may tie or \nsurpass multiple records across Northwest and North Central \nOhio. See the climate section below for details. \n\nChances of rain have slowed down on Tuesday with a slight chance \nacross Lake Erie Tuesday morning and then pushing inland late \nTuesday as a shortwave moves through the flow aloft and pushes a \ncold front south of Lake Erie late afternoon and evening. \nChances of thunderstorms look focused across Northeast Ohio and \nNorthwest Pennsylvania. Some minimal instability is forecast to \ndevelop ahead of the front with dewpoints in the lower 60s. May \nneed to keep an eye on any thunderstorms that can develop given\na stronger wind field with shear in the 0-6km layer of 45-55 \nknots.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nBy late this week, models are in agreement that a strong trough will \ndrop south out of Canada ushering in the coldest air of the season. \nThe front looks to arrive on Friday with cold air filtering into the \nregion through Saturday. There are some noteworthy differences in \nthe long range pattern by the weekend as the ECMWF does not phase \nthe Canadian trough with an upper level low moving out of the Rocky \nMountains and is more progressive with the colder air quickly \nshifting east. The 00Z GFS and Canadian both indicate there will be \nphasing with the upper low which results in a slower trough and \nperhaps lake effect precipitation. Will need to monitor trends as \nlarge spreads currently exist in temperatures based on timing. It is \npossible that we could see frost and freeze conditions next weekend, \nespecially if high pressure ends up overhead on Saturday night.",
